TASK 2 – Choose the Data Types (4 Marks)
1. Data types, sizes and ranges
C data type sizes depend on the compiler and system. The following table gives typical sizes on a modern 64-bit system; the actual sizes should be verified using sizeof().
Data type	Typical size	Range / purpose
char	1 byte	−128 to 127 or 0 to 255, depending on signedness
unsigned short	2 bytes	0 to 65,535 on the assumed system
int	4 bytes	−2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647
unsigned int	4 bytes	0 to 4,294,967,295
long	4 or 8 bytes	System-dependent
long long	8 bytes	At least −9.22 × 10¹⁸ to 9.22 × 10¹⁸
float	4 bytes	Approximately 6–7 decimal digits of precision
double	8 bytes	Approximately 15–16 decimal digits of precision
uint64_t	8 bytes, if available	0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615

limits.h provides integer limits such as INT_MAX and USHRT_MAX. float.h provides floating-point limits and precision information such as FLT_DIG, DBL_DIG, FLT_MAX and DBL_MAX.

Justification: unsigned short is suitable for a 16-bit meter register. unsigned int can store individual consumption values, while a 64-bit integer storing paise is suitable for accumulating billing amounts across a large district. Use char for a category code and integer household identifiers for record identification.

CODE:


#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <float.h>
#include <stdint.h>

/* The meter register is a 16-bit counter */
typedef unsigned short meter_t;

unsigned int consumption(meter_t previous, meter_t current)
{
    // Conversion back to meter_t applies modulo 65536
    return (meter_t)(current - previous);
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("sizeof(meter_t) = %zu bytes, maximum reading = %u\n",
           sizeof(meter_t), (unsigned)USHRT_MAX);

    printf("Consumption: %u units\n", consumption(1200, 1530));
    printf("Consumption: %u units\n", consumption(65400, 120));

    printf("\nInteger limits:\n");
    printf("INT_MAX = %d\n", INT_MAX);
    printf("UINT_MAX = %u\n", UINT_MAX);
    printf("LLONG_MAX = %lld\n", LLONG_MAX);

    printf("\nFloating-point information:\n");
    printf("sizeof(float) = %zu bytes\n", sizeof(float));
    printf("FLT_DIG = %d\n", FLT_DIG);
    printf("sizeof(double) = %zu bytes\n", sizeof(double));
    printf("DBL_DIG = %d\n", DBL_DIG);

    float float_total = 0.0f;
    uint64_t paise_total = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < 4000; i++) {
        float_total += 0.10f; // Rs. 0.10 per bill
        paise_total += 10;    // 10 paise per bill
    }

    printf("\nFloat total for 4000 bills = Rs. %.2f\n",
           float_total);
    printf("Integer paise total = Rs. %llu.%02llu\n",
           (unsigned long long)(paise_total / 100),
           (unsigned long long)(paise_total % 100));

    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:

sizeof(meter_t) = 2 bytes, maximum reading = 65535
Consumption: 330 units
Consumption: 256 units

Integer limits:
INT_MAX = 2147483647
UINT_MAX = 4294967295
LLONG_MAX = 9223372036854775807

Floating-point information:
sizeof(float) = 4 bytes
FLT_DIG = 6
sizeof(double) = 8 bytes
DBL_DIG = 15

Float total for 4000 bills = Rs. 400.02
Integer paise total = Rs. 400.00




